docs/architecture/wire-compat.md:16:- The decision digest covers the sorted entries (package, target version, reasons) and not the schema version, so a version 1 and a version 2 decision with the same entries share a digest. The digest is verified on every read.
docs/architecture/wire-compat.md:27:- `release-pr` snapshots are built by `create-or-update-release-pr.sh` with a hard-coded `schemaVersion: 2`, while the action's `version` input can pin another binary.
docs/architecture/wire-compat.md:28:- The standalone JSON stdout of `release` and `release execute` flattens the receipt and carries its schema version; the nothing-to-release report is a separate shape with version 1.
docs/architecture/wire-compat.md:38:- `callisto schema --type release-receipt` is the only published release schema, and its `schemaVersion` is an unconstrained integer. `callisto schema --type snapshot` is the status report, not `ReleaseInputSnapshotV1`.
docs/architecture/release.md:110:Stdout JSON reports include `schemaVersion`, `command` and `dryRun`; durable decision, intent, artifact-manifest and receipt files retain their own wire shapes. The CLI's `release-pr verify` reader accepts both the strict bare decision and unchanged decide report, validating the report metadata before passing the payload to the shared domain constructor. Unknown and duplicate fields remain errors; durable wire readers are unchanged. The real-binary action contract exercises this producer/consumer boundary through inline, file and stdin inputs.
docs/architecture/release.md:114:Registry evidence currently confirms a version, with optional checksum/yank fields; it does not establish byte identity. Python completion has two modes. Without a sealed file set (the local route, and a CI route whose manifest seals none) it is judged against the files the executing run builds, matched by name, and digests are compared only for files that run uploaded: a rebuild need not be byte-identical to an earlier attempt. With a sealed set, a build job (`release build-python`, no credentials) builds the files and `release artifact-manifest --seal-python` records each file's name, size and sha256 in the manifest, so the envelope's manifest digest covers them. `release execute` verifies the bytes, builds nothing, uploads those files, and treats any index file under a sealed name with another sha256 as an error (E307). The sealed sets are all or none per plan and each belongs to a Python publish of the intent; the manifest stays at schema version 1 because a manifest without sets is unchanged and an older reader rejects the new field. Artifact size/sha256 validation otherwise applies to declared GitHub assets. A PyPI registry reads its simple index at `url` and uploads to `publish-url` when set, else `url`; both endpoints are part of the registry binding. Authenticated reads and HTML-only indexes are not supported.

